% Appendix: GoLLIE Comparison (D4 response)
% exp_ids: gollie_7b_scierc_zs_100, ontcodec_qwen_ft_s1_scierc_100, ontcodec_llama_ft_s1_scierc_100

\section{GoLLIE Comparison}
\label{app:gollie-comparison}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.2pt}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}l rrrr rr r@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Method}
  & DR\,$\downarrow$ & RV\,$\downarrow$ & ET\,$\downarrow$ & CF
  & R-F1\,$\uparrow$ & E-F1\,$\uparrow$
  & \#Pred \\
\midrule
GoLLIE-7B (ZS)        & 0    & 0 & 0 & 0.83 & 0.0   & 9.17  & 5 \\
OntoCoDec Qwen (FT+S1)  & 0    & 0 & 0 & 0.61 & 43.69 & 66.43 & 1121 \\
OntoCoDec Llama (FT+S1) & 0    & 0 & 0 & 0.62 & 45.47 & 67.09 & 1070 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{GoLLIE-7B vs.\ OntoCoDec on SciERC (100 docs, zero-shot for GoLLIE, LoRA + S1 for OntoCoDec). F1 $\times$ 100.}
\label{tab:gollie-comparison}
\end{table}

GoLLIE-7B achieves zero DR/RV/ET violations by generating only 5 predicted relations across 100 documents (54\% produce empty output), representing a trivial solution where formal correctness is achieved through near-complete abstention.
OntoCoDec achieves comparable violation rates (DR${=}$0\%, lower CF) while maintaining R-F1 43--45\% with ${\sim}$200$\times$ more predictions.
Code-style schema encoding thus trades coverage for formal correctness; OntoCoDec achieves both through decoding-time enforcement rather than training-time internalization.

% Appendix: Quantization Robustness (SF2 response)
% exp_ids: quant_qwen_fp16_scierc_ft_s1, quant_qwen_int4_scierc_ft_s1,
%          quant_llama_fp16_scierc_ft_s1, quant_llama_int4_scierc_ft_s1

\section{Quantization Robustness}
\label{app:quantization}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3.6pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}l rrr rrr@{}}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Qwen3-8B}} & \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{Llama-3.1-8B}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(l){5-7}
& FP16 & INT4 & $\Delta$ & FP16 & INT4 & $\Delta$ \\
\midrule
DR\,(\%)\,$\downarrow$  & 0     & 0     & 0      & 0.09  & 0     & $-$0.09 \\
CF\,(\%)   & 1.18  & 1.10  & $-$0.08 & 0.46  & 0.94  & $+$0.48 \\
R-F1\,$\uparrow$ & 43.77 & 41.71 & $-$2.06 & 45.47 & 43.98 & $-$1.49 \\
E-F1\,$\uparrow$ & 67.05 & 67.05 & 0.00   & 67.76 & 67.54 & $-$0.22 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{FP16 vs.\ INT4 (AWQ) quantization under LoRA + S1 on SciERC (551 docs). F1 $\times$ 100.}
\label{tab:quantization}
\end{table}

INT4 quantization does not affect constraint enforcement: DR remains at 0\% for Qwen and improves from 0.09\% to 0\% for Llama; CF variation ($\pm$0.5\,pp) falls within normal cross-run noise.
R-F1 drops 1.5--2.1\,pp under INT4, consistent with expected quantization quality cost independent of the constraint layers.
This provides indirect evidence that the 70B AWQ INT4 results (\S\ref{sec:70b-results}) are not confounded by quantization artifacts in the constraint enforcement mechanism.

